  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "tO6bV31CsfT864ypc1zPChP4iT8=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:22.528Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Systematic's tool field records no partner platform. It says the stack was not researched and notes instead that the firm owns defence and health products of its own. Its v2 verticals \u2014 defence, healthcare and public \u2014 are the markets those products serve.\n\nFor a mechanical rescore that reads size and slot occupancy, this is the awkward case. The platform slot is neither open nor occupied by a third-party vendor whose grip could be loosened; it is occupied by software the firm sells. Anything placed there would have to compete for room on its own product roadmap, which is a product-management decision, not a procurement one.\n\nThat is why the flag stops short of a verdict and asks for an OSS-portfolio screen first. A defence and public-sector product house is exactly the profile where the screen's answer changes the approach entirely: open-source components inside its own products would suggest one conversation, a wholly proprietary portfolio suggests none.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**4 \u00b7 needs OSS screen**\n- Systematic (DK)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Defence, healthcare, public",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:22.528940Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:22.528945Z"
    }
  },
